==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:attachen<ety:bor<fro:atachier>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|attachen}}, from {{der|en|fro|atachier}}, variant of {{m|fro|estachier||bind}}, derived from {{m|fro|estache||stick}}, from {{der|en|frk|*stakkā}}, {{m|frk|*stakō|t=stick}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*stakô|t=pole, bar, stick, stake}}. {{doublet|en|attack}}. More at {{m|en|stake}}, {{m|en|stack}}.

Displaced native {{ncog|ang|þīedan}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈtæt͡ʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-attach.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ætʃ|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|at|tach}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fasten]], to [[join]] to (literally and figuratively).
#: {{syn|en|connect|annex|affix|unite|Thesaurus:join}}
#: {{ant|en|detach|unfasten|disengage|separate|Thesaurus:disconnect}}
#: {{ux|en|You need to '''attach''' the carabiner to your harness.}}
#: {{ux|en|An officer is '''attached''' to a certain regiment, company, or ship.}}
#* {{RQ:Paley Natural Theology|chapter=Of Mechanical Arrangement in the Human Frame|page=117|passage=[T]he ſhoulder-blade has no bony communication with the trunk, either by a joint, or proceſs, or in any other way. {{...}} It is bedded in the fleſh; '''attached''' only to the muſcles.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|passage=A huge stone, to which the cable on the left bank was '''attached''', was removed years later}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To adhere; to be attached.
#: {{syn|en|cling|stick|Thesaurus:adhere}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1838|author={{w|Henry Brougham, 1st Baron Brougham and Vaux|Henry Brougham}}|title=Political Philosophy|passage=The great interest which '''attaches''' to the mere knowledge of these facts cannot be doubted.}}
# To include an [[attachment]] with a communication (especially an email or other electronic communication).
#: {{ux|en|I've '''attached''' the contract to this email.}}
# To come into legal operation in connection with anything; to [[vest]].
#: {{ux|en|Dower will '''attach'''.}}
# To win the heart of; to connect by ties of love or self-interest; to attract; to fasten or bind by moral influence; with ''to''.
#: {{co|en|'''attached''' to a friend; '''attaching''' others to us by wealth or flattery}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|passage=incapable of '''attaching''' a sensible man}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|Charity|passage=God {{...}} by various ties '''attaches''' man to man.}}
# To connect, in a figurative sense; to ascribe or attribute; to affix; with ''to''.
#: {{syn|en|chalk up to|draw|impute|Thesaurus:ascribe}}
#: {{co|en|to '''attach''' great importance to a particular circumstance}}
#: {{ux|en|Some of the guilt must '''attach''' to the parents.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[take]], [[seize]], or lay [[hold]] of.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|act=IV|scene=iii|lines=351–352|passage=Then homeward every man '''attach''' the hand / Of his fair mistress.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|law}} To [[arrest]], [[seize]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=XII|passage=[[eftsoons|Eftsoones]] the Gard, which on his state did wait, / '''Attacht''' that faitor false, and bound him strait {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=III|scene=ii|passage=Old lord, I cannot blame thee, / Who am myself '''attach'd''' with weariness / To th' dulling of my spirits: sit down, and rest.}}
#* {{RQ:Yonge English History|passage=The earl marshal '''attached''' Gloucester for high treason.}}

